        "text": "You've probably heard about Bitcoin, Ethereum, and some other cryptocurrencies. We said before that cryptocurrencies are digital currencies. So are all digital currencies cryptocurrencies? The answer is no. For example, many central banks are working on creating their own versions of digital currency, collectively referred to as central bank Digital Currencies, or CBDCs. However, these are essentially digital versions of fiat money, and they don't enjoy most of the benefits of cryptocurrencies. They are issued and declared legal tender by a central government, and typically don't use a distributed ledger such as a blockchain to keep a record of transactions.",
